00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.480
And then I'll move on
to doing a quick demo

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.610
just so you can see what
it looks like in real life.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.490
So let's start by
looking at what options

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.365
are available in SQL Server.

00:03:18.365 --> 00:03:21.200
And you can see this
rather nice Venn diagram.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.780
I have three main
areas on there.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.790
There's High
Availability if you want

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.730
to be protecting against
individual instance failures.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.580
There's Disaster
Recovery if you want

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.410
to be protecting against
entire region failures.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:39.760
And there's also Read Scaling
if either your application has

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.610
quite high read
requirements or you

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.970
have a geographically-redundant
app, geographically spread

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:51.610
out, that would benefit
from more data locality.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:55.240
So for high availability, one of
the options you might consider

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.870
is failover clustering.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:01.170
It's a much older technology,
much more widely known.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.210
And especially if you
already have existing SAN

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.430
infrastructure, it
might be a good option

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.980
for you, at least on-prem.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.440
For disaster recovery,
you might have thought

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.420
about using SQL mirroring.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.400
Although, Microsoft
are deprecating that in

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:17.200
favor of the basic
availability group,

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.970
which I'll be talking
about a bit later on.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.830
Log shipping is
probably a better option

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:26.250
if you're just doing
disaster recovery.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.470
It's a lot more
simple to set up.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.630
It has very few requirements.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:34.260
It's essentially just a few
SQL jobs that the run on nodes

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.415
to backup databases
on one instance

00:04:36.415 --> 00:04:38.330
and restore them on another.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:39.750
So it's very easy.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.670
You can also do read scaling
if you really want to.

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:45.450
But I probably wouldn't
use log shipping for that.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.790
If you're trying to do
transactional read scaling,

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.100
log shipping isn't
really a good fit,

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.740
because your clients are going
to be constantly disconnected

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.175
when those restore
operations are happening.

00:04:55.175 --> 00:04:57.300
And there's also going to
be quite a bit of latency

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.650
between the backup
and the restore.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.390
So for transactional
read scaling,

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:05.630
you're probably better off with
something like replication.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:08.420
It's a bit more complex to
set up than log shipping,

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.680
but it is pretty good
at doing read scaling.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.860
And then in the middle,
you have AlwaysOn.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.590
AlwaysOn can actually do
all three of these things.

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:19.380
It can do high availability.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.640
It can do disaster recovery.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.900
And it can do read scaling.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.620
So if you want one technology
that can do all three

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.720
of these things, AlwaysOn
is a really good option.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.460
Before moving on,
I will point out

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.040
that you can actually mix
and match these as well.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.780
So you could have
a failover cluster

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.130
that uses log shipping
for disaster recovery

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:41.872
if you wanted to.

00:05:41.872 --> 00:05:43.330
For the rest of
the session though,

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:45.230
I'm just going to
focus on failover

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:47.250
clustering and AlwaysOn.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:51.790
That's because they're the
high availability options.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:55.080
So let's start by taking a
look at failover clustering.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.870
As I said, it's a
much older technology,

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.220
quite well understood.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.680
And it's a pretty
simple concept.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:04.440
You have an IP
address up there that

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.690
can flow between all of the
instances in your cluster.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:11.140
And that's what your client
will use to access the instance.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:14.760
You have multiple servers
as members of your cluster.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.890
And then to those
servers, you present

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.220
some kind of shared storage.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.630
That shared storage is
typically a SAN LUN.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.520
So you need some existing
SAN infrastructure there.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:26.670
It can also be an
SMB 3 file share

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:30.240
if you're using Windows
Server 2016 or above.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.340
But if you are
using a file share,

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:35.350
you need to make sure that's
highly available as well.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:38.130
And then you put your
databases, both your databases

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.890
and the system databases,
on that shared storage.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.340
This is what it looks
like in normal operation.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:50.550
You have the IP address
on the active instance.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.250
You have the SQL
Services running

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:54.430
on only the active instance.

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:57.620
They're stopped on all
of the passive instances.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:59.690
You have that shared
storage mounted

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:01.680
on the primary instance.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.480
And you have that primary
instance servicing the traffic.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:08.830
When you initiate a
failover, the SQL Services

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.690
are first stopped on
the primary instance.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.750
That process can actually take
a few seconds to complete,

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:17.860
because SQL will wait for
any pending transactions

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:19.500
to complete.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:21.580
But once the services
have stopped,

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:23.860
the shared storage
will be dismounted

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.800
from the primary
active instance.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:31.110
The IP address will be removed
from that instance's NIC.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:34.560
The storage will be mounted
onto a passive server.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.300
And then the IP
address will be added

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.520
to your passive server's NIC.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.060
Finally, the services
will be started up

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:44.050
on the passive server.

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:47.360
Now, the amount of time it takes
for SQL Services to start up

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.590
can be quite variable.

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:52.130
It can also be quite
a long period of time.

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:54.320
When the SQL Services
start up, they

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:58.490
have to read some database and
table metadata into memory.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:01.910
And certain transactions
may need to be replayed.

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:06.740
So you may find that this whole
process could take anything

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.110
from 30 seconds to
complete, if you

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:13.110
have relatively small
databases, up to a few minutes

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:16.160
if your database is
a terabyte in size.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.920
And that's 30 seconds
to a few minutes

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:21.320
that your client's
aren't unable to access

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.150
any of these databases.

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:26.532
So failover itself
can be quite slow.

00:08:26.532 --> 00:08:28.990
So if we go over a few of the
considerations for a failover

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:33.130
cluster, you only require a
single copy of the databases,

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:36.200
because they exist on
that much shared storage.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:38.440
There is a requirement
for shared storage.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:42.664
As I said, that will typically
be a SAN LUN or an SMB 3 file

00:08:42.664 --> 00:08:43.940
share.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.400
The failover can
be relatively slow,

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.580
just because of the process
it has to go through.

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:53.750
You can't span data
centers natively with this.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:57.500
And what I mean by that is there
is nothing in the SQL failover

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:01.470
cluster that will allow
you to span data centers.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:04.580
You can actually achieve this
if your SAN array supports

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:07.820
block-level replication, or
if you're using Windows Server

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:11.930
2016, where you have options for
using block-level replication

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:13.220
within that.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:15.920
But with what you get
with a failover cluster,

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:19.250
this isn't something
you can actually do.

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:21.620
And most passive
servers are really

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.490
just sat there, doing nothing.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:27.580
I kind of need to
caveat that slightly.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:30.740
So the passive servers aren't
unable to service traffic

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:34.010
for an individual
failover cluster instance.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.720
That is only active on
a single node at a time.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.410
But you can actually add
multiple failover cluster

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.220
instances to the
same Windows cluster.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.120
Although, bear in mind
if you do that, then

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.490
you need some kind of
logic in your application

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:49.190
to know which instance to
connect to for the database

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:52.300
you're trying to access.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.660
So now let's move on to
AlwaysOn, specifically

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:57.900
availability groups.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:01.460
This was introduced
in SQL Server 2012.

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:04.430
The standard availability
groups are currently

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:08.450
only available in the
Enterprise Edition of SQL.

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:10.790
Although in the
Standard Edition of SQL,

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:14.060
you can use the basic
availability group.

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:15.860
The basic availability
group is a lot

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.960
like a standard
availability group.

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:20.280
It just has a few
key limitations,

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:24.440
such as only being able to
have a maximum of two servers

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:26.780
as a member of that
availability group

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:30.170
and only being able to have a
single database within there

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:31.080
as well.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.560
You also can't use read scaling
with the basic availability

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.630
group.

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:38.250
Before going into AlwaysOn
in a bit more detail,

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:40.520
first, I just want to
cover a couple of terms,

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:42.970
just so we all
know what they are.

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:46.580
An availability group is the
simplest unit of failover

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:48.770
within an AlwaysOn cluster.

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:51.830
You add your databases
to the availability group

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:54.900
and you failover an
entire availability group,

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:56.930
which means that all the
databases within there

00:10:56.930 --> 00:11:00.540
will be failed over
at the same time.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:03.570
You can only add a database
to a single availability

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.350
group at the time.

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:09.270
But a single SQL instance can
host multiple availability

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:10.720
groups at the same time.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:17.850
An availability group may also
have zero or more listeners.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:21.270
The listener is the access
point that the client will use.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:24.000
It has a DNS name, which
is what the client should

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.320
use to connect.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:29.670
And it also has one
or more dedicated IPs.

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:31.410
A listener can
only be associated

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:34.590
with a single availability
group at the time.

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:36.240
And through
Management Studio, you

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:38.280
can only assign
a single listener

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.380
to an availability group.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:45.020
As I said, an availability group
can have multiple listeners.

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:49.170
But to do that, you have to go
into Failover Cluster Manager,

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:53.220
add the listeners there, and
then run some T-SQL whenever

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:54.840
you perform a failover.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:58.358
If you go to the link on the
screen or scan the QR code,

00:11:58.358 --> 00:12:00.150
it will take you to
some documentation that

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:02.232
shows you how to set those up.

00:12:02.232 --> 00:12:04.440
You may be wondering why
you want multiple listeners,

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:06.110
and I'll talk about
that a bit later on.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.600
This is what
AlwaysOn looks like.

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.360
You still have the
single listener

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:17.930
that can float between all of
the servers in the cluster.

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:20.900
You still have multiple
servers in the cluster.

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:22.550
But the key difference
here is that you

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:24.810
don't have any shared storage.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:28.760
Each of these servers has
its own set of local storage.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:32.270
On that local storage,
you add your databases

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:35.900
or you add your databases
to the availability group.

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:38.090
And then those databases
will be replicated

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:42.920
to the storage on all of the
instances within the cluster.

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:47.420
However, what isn't replicated
are any other system databases.

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:48.910
So when you install
a SQL instance

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:52.000
in an AlwaysOn cluster, what
you actually just installed

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.620
is a normal SQL instance.

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:56.800
The same way you would if
you weren't using AlwaysOn.

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:59.440
It does mean that you can
use GC Images to build these,

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:03.420
by the way, and then add
them to a cluster afterwards.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:06.630
So because the system
database is unreplicated,

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:09.810
that means anything that
you add to a system database

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:13.620
needs to be added to all of
these instances individually.

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:17.550
So SQL log-ins, SQL jobs,
they don't get replicated.

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:19.500
You somehow have to
manage adding them

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:23.000
to all of these instances.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:26.350
This is what AlwaysOn
looks like in service.

00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:29.860
The listener is on
the active instance.

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:31.960
The services this
time are running

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:35.770
on all of the servers in the
cluster at the same time.

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:38.690
They're not stopped on
any of the passive ones.

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:42.110
And you have the primary
database in read/write mode.

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:45.530
You also have the secondary
database in read-only mode.

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:49.390
And then you have the primary
database replicating over

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:52.240
to the secondary database.

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:55.450
When you initiate a failover,
the primary database

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:58.810
is marked as read-only, the
direction of replication

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:03.740
is split, the secondary database
is marked as read write,

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:07.597
and the listener is moved
to the secondary instance.

00:14:07.597 --> 00:14:08.180
And that's it.

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:10.380
That's a failover complete.

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:12.352
Now, you may think
that looks quite quick.

00:14:12.352 --> 00:14:14.060
Actually, this is a
really quick process.

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:17.590
It probably will take you a
few seconds most of the time.

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:20.980
What's even better is that is
an actually predictable amount

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:22.030
of time.

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:23.530
There is a
configuration parameter

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:25.660
you can set in your
availability group

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:30.210
to say how long this failover
should take to complete.

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:33.490
Now, I did mention you can also
use an AlwaysOn availability

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:35.170
group for read scaling.

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:37.340
And this is how you would do it.

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:41.230
So for normal client request
to an availability group,

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:43.240
the client will connect
to the listener.

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:46.840
The listener will connect the
client to the active instance.

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:50.490
And then the active instance
will service the request.

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:52.530
If you want to use read-only--

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:54.320
if you want to use
read scaling, you

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:57.550
need to take advantage
of read-only routing.

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:01.400
To do that, you specify this
in your connection string,

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:04.250
ApplicationIntent=ReadOnly.

00:15:04.250 --> 00:15:06.590
The client will still
connect to the listener.

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:08.450
The listener will still
connect the client

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:10.487
to the primary instance.

00:15:10.487 --> 00:15:13.070
However, the difference here is
that the primary instance will

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:16.310
see that this has been specified
in the connection string,

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:19.220
and rather than servicing
the request itself,

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:22.040
it will consult its
read-only routing list

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:24.810
to determine which instance
should service the request.

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:29.650
Now, the read-only
routing list effectively

00:15:29.650 --> 00:15:32.850
says that if this particular
instance is active,

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:36.660
then these instances are able
to service read requests for it.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:38.520
So you could have a
read-only routing list

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:41.040
that says whenever
SQL 1 is active,

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:45.235
then both SQL 2 and SQL 3 are
able to service a request.

00:15:45.235 --> 00:15:47.500
And you could have a
second entry in there

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:49.950
that says that if
SQL 2 is active,

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:54.820
then only SQL 1 is able
to service that request.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:57.360
So once the active
instance has consulted

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:59.040
its read-only
routing list, it'll

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:01.800
pick an instance for the
client to connect to.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:04.570
It will tell the client to
connect to that instance.

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:07.168
And then the client, or
the secondary instance,

00:16:07.168 --> 00:16:08.210
will service the request.

00:16:10.993 --> 00:16:12.660
So these are the
considerations that you

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:16.150
have for AlwaysOn generally.

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:19.450
Each node has to have a
copy of the databases on it,

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:20.890
so your storage
requirements might

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:23.310
be increased by using this.

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:25.700
However, there is no
requirement for shared storage,

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:29.000
so you can actually get away
with using cheaper storage.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:31.460
The failover is relatively
fast and can actually

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:33.290
be predictable.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:36.200
And you can natively span
data centers with this.

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:39.710
The underlying technology here
is essentially SQL mirroring.

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:42.020
And because of that,
your SQL instances

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:43.820
can actually be anywhere.

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:44.900
They could be on-prem.

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:46.850
They could be in
another cloud provider.

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:48.920
They could be in
another GCP region.

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:50.550
It doesn't really matter.

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:52.790
You can put them anywhere.

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:55.323
And finally, those secondary
instances can get some use.

00:16:55.323 --> 00:16:56.740
They can be used
as read replicas.

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:04.148
So what would I recommend
you actually use in GCP?

00:17:04.148 --> 00:17:05.940
And this has kind of
changed slightly given

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:08.020
the announcement yesterday.

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:10.560
So I'd say if what you're
looking for is highly available

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:13.980
SQL and you have very
standard requirements,

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:15.660
then actually the
managed Cloud SQL

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:19.599
offering might be a pretty
compelling offer, because it's

00:17:19.599 --> 00:17:20.680
a PaaS option.

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:23.800
You won't have so much
management overhead.

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:27.310
The only problem is you won't
get as much flexibility.

00:17:27.310 --> 00:17:29.730
So if you wanted to
do high availability

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:31.860
and still maintain the
flexibility of managing

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.050
your own SQL
instances, you really

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:38.190
have the choice between failover
clustering and AlwaysOn.

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:40.110
The problem with
failover clustering

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:42.720
is this requirement
for shared storage.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:45.720
In GCP, a persistent
disk can only

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:50.770
be mounted to a single instance
at a time in read/write mode.

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:54.480
That means that you can't
really use shared storage in GCP

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.920
for a failover cluster.

00:17:56.920 --> 00:18:01.810
You could create-- you could
use the block level replication

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:06.190
options within Windows Server
2016 to get around that.

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:08.550
But then you're adding
additional management overhead

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:10.803
and additional
complexity to this.

00:18:10.803 --> 00:18:12.220
And really, you
might as well just

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:14.950
use AlwaysOn from the
start, because it's designed

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:17.880
for this type of scenario.

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:19.440
The one thing you
may be considering

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:22.680
there is the licensing
cost, because as I said,

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:24.510
for a standard
availability group,

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:27.720
you have to use the
Enterprise Edition of SQL.

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:31.350
That means you're paying a
much larger licensing cost.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.390
However, you may
be able to get away

00:18:33.390 --> 00:18:35.610
with using the basic
availability group.

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:38.640
You may find that the
limitations don't really

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:41.330
affect you that much.

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:43.940
So now, let's take a look
at what AlwaysOn actually

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:45.275
looks like within GCP.

00:18:49.510 --> 00:18:50.950
So what I'm going
to build up here

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:53.800
is a diagram that shows
you a single region

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:55.090
deployment of AlwaysOn.

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:57.910
After building that
diagram up, I'll

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:00.070
explain what slight
differences there

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:03.920
would be with a
multi-region deployment.

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:05.770
So you'll start by
picking a region.

00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:08.410
That region should have
at least three zones.

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:10.870
And then you'll pick three
of those zones to use.

00:19:10.870 --> 00:19:15.091
I've called them zone
A, zone B, and zone C.

00:19:15.091 --> 00:19:17.890
You'll then create
three subnets.

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:22.670
One of those subnets will be
your shared services subnet.

00:19:22.670 --> 00:19:25.640
That will have instances
deployed into it in all three

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:27.870
of these zones.

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:30.030
You'll then have a
second subnet, which

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:32.280
I've called the SQL 1 subnet.

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:35.150
That will have a single
instance deployed into it.

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:38.250
And that instance
will be in zone A.

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:41.180
And then you'll create
your third subnet.

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:44.930
That again will have a single
instance deployed into it.

00:19:44.930 --> 00:19:48.640
And that instance
will be in zone C.

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:52.900
You'll then create two domain
controllers, both in subnet 1,

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:56.680
with one of them in zone A
and one of them in zone C.

00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:00.310
The reason we create them
in those zones is to avoid--

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:05.350
is to minimize the intrazone
traffic between the SQL

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:08.170
instances and the
domain controllers.

00:20:08.170 --> 00:20:11.380
Although, again, given Google
announced managed AD yesterday,

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:15.100
that might be a good
option to use as well.

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:17.310
You'll also create
a file share witness

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:20.340
in zone B. The file share
witness is effectively

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:21.930
just a file server.

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:24.030
And all it's doing is
providing core room

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:27.840
for the cluster to avoid any
sort of split brain scenarios.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:30.090
The reason we're
deploying it into zone B

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:32.700
is because a mark
of best practice

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:36.120
is to keep your file share
witness in a zone that doesn't

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:38.100
have any SQL services in it.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:42.600
That's to avoid any sort of
network partitioning effects.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:45.940
Once you've done that, you'll
create two SQL instances.

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:48.630
One of those SQL instances
will be in subnet 2.

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:51.150
The other one will
be in subnet 3.

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:53.550
Those SQL instances
will have their own set

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:55.320
of persistent disks.

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:59.610
They will also have an alias
IP range specified on them.

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:02.110
Now, if you don't know
what an alias IP range is,

00:21:02.110 --> 00:21:04.480
it's effectively a
subnet of the subnet

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:06.700
that the instance
is deployed in.

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:09.160
And it tells GCP
that any requests

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:13.270
for IPs within
the alias IP range

00:21:13.270 --> 00:21:15.503
should be sent to that instant.

00:21:15.503 --> 00:21:17.670
So it just means you can
add additional IP addresses

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:18.990
to that instance.

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:23.090
And GCP will know how
to route the requests.

00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:25.880
You'll then create
a Windows Cluster.

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.640
That Windows Cluster will
have the two SQL instances

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:29.960
as members.

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:33.650
It will also have the file
share witness as its core.

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:36.770
And it will have some core
cluster resources in there

00:21:36.770 --> 00:21:38.540
as well.

00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:41.420
Those cluster resources
will have a DNS name.

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:45.330
And they will also have two
IP addresses in this case.

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:47.900
That's because those
core cluster resources

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:49.910
will have an IP
address from each

00:21:49.910 --> 00:21:54.110
of the alias IP ranges that's
specified on the SQL instance.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:56.810
What this means is that
that resource can be hosted

00:21:56.810 --> 00:21:58.730
on either of these instances.

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:00.860
Failover Cluster
Manager will check

00:22:00.860 --> 00:22:05.120
to see if there is an IP
that is in the same subnet

00:22:05.120 --> 00:22:07.660
as the primary IP of the
instance you're failing over

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:08.270
to.

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:12.880
And if there is, it will
bring that resource online.

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:14.890
You'll then create some
availability groups.

00:22:14.890 --> 00:22:17.020
In this case, I've created two.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:19.270
Those availability
groups will have

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:21.280
some databases within them.

00:22:21.280 --> 00:22:22.930
Those availability
groups can also

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:25.180
be failed over independently.

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:28.630
And the availability groups
will have their own listeners.

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:31.270
Again, the listeners
will have a DNS name.

00:22:31.270 --> 00:22:34.930
And they will have two IPs,
one from each of the alias IP

00:22:34.930 --> 00:22:39.060
ranges on the SQL instances.

00:22:39.060 --> 00:22:42.740
So that's the single region
deployment of AlwaysOn.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:44.930
With the multi-region
deployment,

00:22:44.930 --> 00:22:46.633
it looks quite similar.

00:22:46.633 --> 00:22:48.050
There are only a
couple of changes

00:22:48.050 --> 00:22:50.090
that you'd actually make.

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:52.450
The first is you would deploy
some domain controllers

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:55.300
into that region as well,
then create a shared services

00:22:55.300 --> 00:22:58.420
subnet within that region.

00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:02.510
You'd create at least two domain
controllers for redundancy.

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:06.650
You'll also create additional
SQL instances in that region.

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:10.550
And with those SQL instances,
you will create them

00:23:10.550 --> 00:23:14.330
each in their own subnet, and
you will specify an alias IP

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:15.840
range on them.

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:20.640
You'll then add additional IPs
to all of the cluster resources

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:24.090
from those alias IP
ranges you specified.

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:25.950
Effectively, your
cluster resources

00:23:25.950 --> 00:23:28.650
should have the same
number of IP addresses

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:33.660
as you have SQL instances
within that cluster.

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:35.780
Finally, the file share
witness should actually

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:38.270
be moved to its own
region, because if you

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:41.690
have a multi-region deployment,
the best practice there

00:23:41.690 --> 00:23:44.480
is to ensure it's deployed
into a region that doesn't have

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:46.580
any SQL services within it.

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:49.290
Again, that's to avoid any
sort of network partitioning

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:49.790
effects.

00:23:53.795 --> 00:23:55.170
So one of the
things you're going

00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:58.440
to have to choose when you
create the availability group

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:00.960
is the availability mode.

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:05.400
You've got two choices here;
synchronous and asynchronous.

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:07.680
With the synchronous
availability mode,

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:11.260
transactions are only committed
on the primary database

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:13.320
once the secondary
databases have

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:16.860
confirmed that the transactions
have been committed on them.

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:20.273
This is really good from a
data consistency perspective,

00:24:20.273 --> 00:24:21.690
because it means
you can guarantee

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:25.820
that the secondaries are up
to date with the primary.

00:24:25.820 --> 00:24:28.620
However, from a write
performance perspective,

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:30.022
it's not quite so good.

00:24:30.022 --> 00:24:32.480
Because your primary is having
to wait for your secondaries

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:34.490
to commit transactions,
you'll actually

00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:38.010
find that your write
performance is decreased.

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:39.470
So you should only
really use this

00:24:39.470 --> 00:24:43.190
when the latency between the
instances is relatively low

00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:48.050
and data consistency is
your primary concern.

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:52.400
Asynchronous availability
mode, on the other hand,

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:54.770
commits the
transactions immediately

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:58.340
on the primary database, and
then the primary database

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:01.430
doesn't really care what
happens with the secondaries.

00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:03.680
It's really good from a write
performance perspective,

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:06.900
because there is no impact
on your write performance.

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:10.070
However, it's not quite so
good from a data consistency

00:25:10.070 --> 00:25:13.520
perspective, because
if you try to failover

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:15.350
to one of these
instances, there's

00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:17.790
a chance you might lose data.

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:20.040
Now, the good news is
this isn't a choice

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:22.860
that you have to make at the
availability group level.

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:25.200
You can configure this
on each of the instances

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:28.230
within the availability group.

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:31.550
So the way that I would
typically see this being used

00:25:31.550 --> 00:25:34.010
is that you would have
your primary region.

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:37.070
And the instances that you
deploy into your primary region

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:40.220
will use the synchronous
availability mode.

00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:42.980
The reason for this is that the
latency between those instances

00:25:42.980 --> 00:25:47.660
is going to be relatively low,
tens of milliseconds maybe.

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:51.500
And you're most likely to be
failing over between instances

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:54.470
within the same region.

00:25:54.470 --> 00:25:57.230
If you deploy SQL instances
to any other regions,

00:25:57.230 --> 00:26:01.750
you'll use the asynchronous
availability mode.

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:03.250
The reason for
that is that you're

00:26:03.250 --> 00:26:06.250
unlikely to be failing
over to that region

00:26:06.250 --> 00:26:07.970
unless there's a disaster.

00:26:07.970 --> 00:26:09.880
And if there's a
disaster, you can probably

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:11.800
afford some data loss.

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:15.460
And the latency between the
instances in different regions

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:17.050
is relatively high.

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:19.030
You're looking at
hundreds of milliseconds

00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:21.140
as opposed to tens.

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:24.710
So if you were to try and
use synchronous availability

00:26:24.710 --> 00:26:26.690
across regions,
you'll probably find

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:29.930
that your write performance
is affected a bit too much.

00:26:33.890 --> 00:26:35.560
So connectivity
is the next thing

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:37.630
you might be thinking
about, especially

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:41.380
because these listeners
might have multiple IPs.

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:45.412
So how exactly the clients
connect to those instances.

00:26:45.412 --> 00:26:46.870
Well, as I said,
the clients should

00:26:46.870 --> 00:26:49.210
connect using the DNS name.

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:51.250
And then the default
configuration,

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:55.000
the Failover Cluster Manager
will register every possible IP

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:58.040
address within DNS.

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:00.180
If you have an
AlwaysOn-aware client,

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:02.410
you should use this in
your connection string,

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:05.620
MultiSubnetFailover=True.

00:27:05.620 --> 00:27:07.450
That will tell the
client to attempt

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:11.120
to connect to all of those IP
addresses at the same time.

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:14.600
And as soon as one of the
connections is successful,

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:17.240
it will stop trying to
connect to the rest of them.

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:21.180
This allows failover to
be as fast as possible.

00:27:21.180 --> 00:27:23.430
Legacy clients,
unfortunately, are

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:26.360
going to get back a
list of IP addresses.

00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:28.830
They're going to pick one
at random to connect to.

00:27:28.830 --> 00:27:31.330
And if that happens to be an
IP address that isn't currently

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:34.870
online, your client's
going to get a timeout.

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:36.810
So if you do have
a legacy client,

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:40.150
you should configure these
two parameters on the listener

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:41.820
cluster resource.

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:45.060
The first is
RegisterAllProvidersIP,

00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:47.160
which you should set to 0.

00:27:47.160 --> 00:27:49.020
That tells the client
that it should only

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:53.430
register the currently
active IP address in DNS.

00:27:53.430 --> 00:27:56.580
You should also lower
the HostRecordTTL.

00:27:56.580 --> 00:27:58.775
By default, that's
set to 20 minutes.

00:27:58.775 --> 00:28:00.150
And I don't know
about you, but I

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:02.610
don't want to be waiting
20 minutes for my client

00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:03.957
to failover.

00:28:03.957 --> 00:28:05.790
You should probably
reduce that to something

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:07.200
a lot more sensible.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:09.030
Maybe start at 2
minutes, and see

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:10.440
if that's all right for you.

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:12.340
If not, lower it a bit more.

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:15.840
Although, do bear in mind,
the lower the HostRecordTTL,

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:20.020
the more DNS traffic is going
to hit your domain controllers.

00:28:20.020 --> 00:28:23.440
If you do you want to be able
to configure these listener

00:28:23.440 --> 00:28:26.230
parameters, you can go to
the link on the screen,

00:28:26.230 --> 00:28:28.178
and it will take you
to get Github Gist that

00:28:28.178 --> 00:28:30.220
will give you a PowerShell
snippet that shows you

00:28:30.220 --> 00:28:30.850
how to do that.

00:28:33.790 --> 00:28:35.100
What about Linux?

00:28:35.100 --> 00:28:36.590
We're at a Google conference.

00:28:36.590 --> 00:28:38.952
We should talk about
Linux at some point.

00:28:38.952 --> 00:28:40.660
So the good news is
that you can actually

00:28:40.660 --> 00:28:42.490
run SQL Server on Linux.

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:45.622
And you've been able to
since SQL Server 2016.

00:28:45.622 --> 00:28:47.330
If you really wanted
to, you could run it

00:28:47.330 --> 00:28:50.340
in a Docker container as well.

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:55.490
Also, since SQL Server 2016,
and in fact Windows Server 2016,

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:59.510
domain-independent availability
groups have been a possibility.

00:28:59.510 --> 00:29:01.970
So in Windows Server
2016, Microsoft

00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:04.880
introduced the
domain-independent cluster.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:07.820
Previously, all the
members of a cluster

00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:09.860
had to be part of
the same domain,

00:29:09.860 --> 00:29:14.510
but in Windows Server 2016
that limitation was removed.

00:29:14.510 --> 00:29:16.580
And members of a
cluster could be

00:29:16.580 --> 00:29:19.970
from different domains,
different forests, or even just

00:29:19.970 --> 00:29:22.480
were group joined.

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:24.670
The domain-independent
availability group

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:28.120
was introduced in
SQL Server 2016 just

00:29:28.120 --> 00:29:31.090
as a way of creating
availability groups on top

00:29:31.090 --> 00:29:34.540
of those domain-independent
clusters.

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:37.260
Now, this was brought along
to SQL Server on Linux

00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:39.570
in SQL Server 2017.

00:29:39.570 --> 00:29:41.970
And you're actually able to
create availability groups

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:44.422
on top of Pacemaker clusters.

00:29:44.422 --> 00:29:47.840
You may also be wondering if
you can mix Windows and Linux

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:51.030
servers within the same
availability group.

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:52.875
And the answer is
that you sort of can.

00:29:52.875 --> 00:29:54.500
The issue with it is
that you don't get

00:29:54.500 --> 00:29:56.540
any sort of high availability.

00:29:56.540 --> 00:29:59.090
And that's just because there's
no common Cluster Manager

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:02.785
to mediate the failover
of the IP address.

00:30:02.785 --> 00:30:04.410
If you go through a
link on the screen,

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:06.020
there's a lot more
detail about how

00:30:06.020 --> 00:30:07.610
you would set this up on Linux.

00:30:11.240 --> 00:30:13.040
Now, you may be
wondering about backups.

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:14.907
It's quite an important topic.

00:30:14.907 --> 00:30:16.490
And it's not really
one that I'm going

00:30:16.490 --> 00:30:18.660
to get into a huge
amount of detail today,

00:30:18.660 --> 00:30:21.542
mainly because it's probably
a session in its own right.

00:30:21.542 --> 00:30:23.000
I will give you a
couple of options

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:24.990
that you could look, though.

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:29.180
The first is creating some SQL
jobs on each of the instances

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:32.640
within your cluster, putting
some logic in that job

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:36.120
to ensure it only runs on a
single instance at a time,

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:39.120
potentially just by seeing if
that instance is the currently

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:41.790
active one for the
availability group,

00:30:41.790 --> 00:30:44.040
and then having it
set within a SQL job

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:47.370
to offload that backup
onto cloud storage.

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:49.440
There are also a number
of third party products

00:30:49.440 --> 00:30:53.262
that you can use that will be
able to back up these databases

00:30:53.262 --> 00:30:53.970
to cloud storage.

00:30:56.870 --> 00:31:00.950
So I've gone about half an hour.

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:03.630
And I'm a reliability engineer.

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:06.813
And this is the first time
I've talked about monitoring.

00:31:06.813 --> 00:31:08.730
Again, I'm not going to
go into lots of detail

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:11.250
about monitoring, mainly
because this is probably

00:31:11.250 --> 00:31:14.080
an entire conference
in its own right.

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:17.190
However, I will say that the
Stackdriver Monitoring agent

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:18.990
can be installed on Windows.

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:20.820
And when it is
installed on Windows,

00:31:20.820 --> 00:31:24.410
it's able to gather
SQL Server metrics.

00:31:24.410 --> 00:31:26.360
The Stackdriver
Logging agent can also

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:27.970
be installed on Windows.

00:31:27.970 --> 00:31:30.380
And by default, that
will collect only

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:32.160
the Windows event logs.

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:35.190
SQL Server does log some
things to the event log.

00:31:35.190 --> 00:31:38.700
So you may be able to just
get away with logging those.

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:40.790
But you can also configure
Stackdriver Logging

00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:45.070
to ingest text-based logs,
which SQL Server also keeps.

00:31:45.070 --> 00:31:47.180
So Stackdriver Logging
would be a good option

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:49.920
for any sort of log management.

00:31:49.920 --> 00:31:52.910
Finally, if endpoint health is
something you want to monitor,

00:31:52.910 --> 00:31:54.740
Stackdriver monitoring
does actually

00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:57.170
give you the option of
using uptime checks.

00:31:57.170 --> 00:31:59.300
So you could see if that
would suit your purpose.

00:32:02.320 --> 00:32:04.150
So finally for this
section, let's just

00:32:04.150 --> 00:32:09.260
go over some considerations
for AlwaysOn in GCP.

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:11.650
Each SQL Server
Compute Engine instance

00:32:11.650 --> 00:32:14.160
requires its own subnet.

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:17.530
The file share witness should
be in a zone in a single region

00:32:17.530 --> 00:32:22.810
deployment or a region in a
multi-region deployment that

00:32:22.810 --> 00:32:26.290
has no SQL instances in it.

00:32:26.290 --> 00:32:29.230
Active Directory isn't
actually a requirement anymore,

00:32:29.230 --> 00:32:32.290
but I still highly recommend
that you include it.

00:32:32.290 --> 00:32:35.330
Mainly because it makes
management a lot easier.

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:38.200
And if you've ever tried setting
up certificate authentication

00:32:38.200 --> 00:32:40.210
for SQL Server,
you'll probably wish

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:43.720
you had Active Directory
to use instead.

00:32:43.720 --> 00:32:46.420
Synchronous replication
should be used for instances

00:32:46.420 --> 00:32:48.490
that are in the same region.

00:32:48.490 --> 00:32:50.740
And conversely,
asynchronous replication

00:32:50.740 --> 00:32:54.830
should be used for instances
that are in different regions.

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:56.500
So now let's move
on to a quick demo

00:32:56.500 --> 00:32:58.208
just so you can see
what this looks like.

00:33:03.338 --> 00:33:06.530
A quick demo.

00:33:06.530 --> 00:33:08.610
There we go.

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:10.670
So this is a GCP console.

00:33:10.670 --> 00:33:14.600
You can see that there are
a number of instances here.

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:18.500
I have two domain controllers,
a file share witness,

00:33:18.500 --> 00:33:21.310
and two SQL instances.

00:33:21.310 --> 00:33:25.330
All of these instances
are in the same network,

00:33:25.330 --> 00:33:28.250
and you can see that the two
domain controllers and the file

00:33:28.250 --> 00:33:31.120
share witness are all
in the same subnet.

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:34.330
The two SQL instances
are in different subnets,

00:33:34.330 --> 00:33:36.690
as you can see here.

00:33:36.690 --> 00:33:39.830
If I take a look
at SQL 1, you can

00:33:39.830 --> 00:33:44.720
see that it has an alias
IP range configured.

00:33:44.720 --> 00:33:48.490
And that alias IP range falls
within the range of the subnet

00:33:48.490 --> 00:33:51.150
that this instance is in.

00:33:51.150 --> 00:33:58.660
If I switch over to SQL
1 and reconnect to SQL 1,

00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:02.760
this is what Failover Cluster
Manager would look like.

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:06.260
You can see that the core
cluster resources are currently

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:08.370
running on SQL 1.

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:10.610
And if I scroll
down, you can see

00:34:10.610 --> 00:34:13.670
that there is a resource
for the file share witness,

00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:16.940
as well as a resource for the
DNS name for the core cluster

00:34:16.940 --> 00:34:18.150
resources.

00:34:18.150 --> 00:34:19.880
And if I expand
that, you can see

00:34:19.880 --> 00:34:21.889
that the IP address
that's online

00:34:21.889 --> 00:34:26.239
is the one from the
same subnet as SQL 1.

00:34:26.239 --> 00:34:30.500
If I switch the roles that are
running within this cluster,

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:32.420
I have two availability groups.

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:35.870
ag1 is currently
running on SQL 1,

00:34:35.870 --> 00:34:39.830
and ag2 is currently
running on SQL 2.

00:34:39.830 --> 00:34:42.545
If I look at the
resources within ag1,

00:34:42.545 --> 00:34:44.920
you can see that there is a
resource for the availability

00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:45.830
group.

00:34:45.830 --> 00:34:48.530
There's also a resource
for the listener.

00:34:48.530 --> 00:34:50.949
And if I expand
the listener, you

00:34:50.949 --> 00:34:53.889
can see that the IP
address that's online

00:34:53.889 --> 00:34:57.290
is in the same subnet as SQL 1.

00:34:57.290 --> 00:34:59.450
If I look at the
same thing for ag2,

00:34:59.450 --> 00:35:01.520
you'll see the same resources.

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:04.790
The only difference here is that
the IP address that's online

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:07.502
is in SQL 2's subnet.

00:35:07.502 --> 00:35:09.210
So if I switch over
to Management Studio,

00:35:09.210 --> 00:35:13.290
you can see that SQL 1
has four databases on it.

00:35:13.290 --> 00:35:16.910
All four databases are in
the synchronized state.

00:35:16.910 --> 00:35:20.210
And SQL 2 has the same
four databases, again,

00:35:20.210 --> 00:35:22.706
all in the synchronized state.

00:35:22.706 --> 00:35:27.300
If I take a look at the
availability groups on SQL 1,

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:32.600
you'll see that SQL 1 is primary
for ag1 and secondary for ag2.

00:35:32.600 --> 00:35:34.680
If I take a look
at SQL 2, you'll

00:35:34.680 --> 00:35:38.165
see it's the other way around.

00:35:38.165 --> 00:35:40.040
So let's take a look at
the properties of one

00:35:40.040 --> 00:35:43.090
of these availability groups.

00:35:43.090 --> 00:35:48.640
You can see that ag1 has two
databases in it; db1 and db2.

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:52.450
It also has two instances as
a member of the availability

00:35:52.450 --> 00:35:53.680
group.

00:35:53.680 --> 00:35:56.620
Those instances are both
using the synchronous commit

00:35:56.620 --> 00:35:58.120
availability mode.

00:35:58.120 --> 00:36:00.710
And I can actually easily
change this to asynchronous

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:04.850
if I want to just by
selecting it here.

00:36:04.850 --> 00:36:08.250
They're also set to use the
automatic failover mode.

00:36:08.250 --> 00:36:11.080
That just means that if one
of these instances fails,

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:14.200
the other can automatically
pick up from it.

00:36:14.200 --> 00:36:16.900
I can change that to
manual if I would like to.

00:36:16.900 --> 00:36:19.770
And in fact, if I'm using
the asynchronous commit mode,

00:36:19.770 --> 00:36:22.800
then manual is my only option.

00:36:22.800 --> 00:36:25.400
You can also see that
the instances are

00:36:25.400 --> 00:36:27.840
set to be readable secondaries.

00:36:27.840 --> 00:36:30.030
If I take a look at the
read-only routing list,

00:36:30.030 --> 00:36:32.630
you can see that there
are two instances.

00:36:32.630 --> 00:36:34.470
They each have their
own read-only routing

00:36:34.470 --> 00:36:38.960
URL, which tells the
client where to connect to.

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:41.170
And then they have their
own individual read-only

00:36:41.170 --> 00:36:43.970
routing list.

00:36:43.970 --> 00:36:47.620
So now, let's try doing a
failover and see what happens.

00:36:47.620 --> 00:36:49.940
So I actually have
an app running here.

00:36:49.940 --> 00:36:51.070
It's very simple.

00:36:51.070 --> 00:36:53.640
All it's doing is
every 5 seconds,

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:56.110
it's connecting to
each of the listeners,

00:36:56.110 --> 00:37:00.340
running the SELECT
@@SERVERNAME SQL statement,

00:37:00.340 --> 00:37:03.110
and just returning the
value of that command.

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:06.440
So that would tell us which
instance is currently active.

00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:10.480
It also would give us a count
of the errors that are found

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:13.820
when attempting the connection.

00:37:13.820 --> 00:37:19.550
So if we initiate failover
of ag1, all I need to do

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:21.140
is step through the wizard.

00:37:21.140 --> 00:37:23.037
Select which instance
to failover to.

00:37:23.037 --> 00:37:24.620
You can see in this
case there's going

00:37:24.620 --> 00:37:27.530
to be no data lost, because I'm
using synchronous commit mode.

00:37:30.370 --> 00:37:32.660
Connect to the instance
I'm going to failover to.

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:37.760
And then wait a few seconds
for the failover to complete.

00:37:41.158 --> 00:37:41.700
There you go.

00:37:41.700 --> 00:37:42.742
The failover is complete.

00:37:45.310 --> 00:37:50.230
You can see that SQL 1
is now secondary for ag1.

00:37:50.230 --> 00:37:53.260
SQL 2 is primary for ag1.

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:55.600
And if I go to Failover
Cluster Manager,

00:37:55.600 --> 00:37:59.480
you can see that the
owner node is now SQL 2.

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:04.270
And the IP address that's online
is the one in SQL 2's subnet.

00:38:04.270 --> 00:38:07.150
If I go to the app, you can see
that the active instance is now

00:38:07.150 --> 00:38:08.370
SQL 2.

00:38:08.370 --> 00:38:11.742
And there was actually an
error during that failover.

00:38:11.742 --> 00:38:13.325
Let's try the same
thing with ag2.

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:19.430
So again, we initiate
the failover and step

00:38:19.430 --> 00:38:22.070
through the wizard.

00:38:22.070 --> 00:38:24.215
Wait a few seconds for
the wizard to complete.

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:30.390
And there we go.

00:38:30.390 --> 00:38:32.070
ag2 is failed over.

00:38:32.070 --> 00:38:36.090
Again, ag2 is now
primary on SQL 1.

00:38:36.090 --> 00:38:42.090
And if we refresh SQL 2,
we'll see that ag2 is now

00:38:42.090 --> 00:38:43.740
secondary on there.

00:38:43.740 --> 00:38:46.360
Again, we go to Failover
Cluster Manager.

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:47.970
Check ag2.

00:38:47.970 --> 00:38:49.980
The owner node is SQL 1.

00:38:49.980 --> 00:38:53.730
And the IP address that's
online is in SQL 1's subnet.

00:38:53.730 --> 00:38:55.200
If we go back over
to the app, you

00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:59.490
can see that SQL 1 is now
the active instance for ag2.

00:38:59.490 --> 00:39:03.305
And there were no errors
during that failover.

00:39:03.305 --> 00:39:04.680
Now, it's worth
pointing out here

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:08.220
that actually it's normal to
get a connection failure when

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:09.725
the failover is completed.

00:39:09.725 --> 00:39:11.850
And that's usually during
the point at which the IP

00:39:11.850 --> 00:39:14.890
address is moved to one instance
to another, or in this case,

00:39:14.890 --> 00:39:16.890
the IP address changes.

00:39:16.890 --> 00:39:18.420
What you should
do in your app is

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:20.190
to have some kind
of logic in there

00:39:20.190 --> 00:39:22.540
to reattempt the connection.

00:39:22.540 --> 00:39:25.110
And you'll probably find
that on the second attempt,

00:39:25.110 --> 00:39:26.950
it will probably be successful.

00:39:26.950 --> 00:39:30.300
So use something like an
exponential backoff for it.

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:31.670
So that's it for the demo.

00:39:31.670 --> 00:39:35.960
If we go back to the
slides, I did have a video

00:39:35.960 --> 00:39:38.378
here just in case the demo
gods didn't smile on me.

00:39:38.378 --> 00:39:39.545
But thankfully, that worked.

00:39:39.545 --> 00:39:42.130
So I can skip over that.

00:39:42.130 --> 00:39:43.880
If you go to the
link on the screen,

00:39:43.880 --> 00:39:46.240
you will find some
step-by-step instructions

00:39:46.240 --> 00:39:48.920
for how to set this up in GCP.

00:39:48.920 --> 00:39:50.420
There will be some
G Cloud commands.

00:39:50.420 --> 00:39:52.920
There will be some
PowerShell snippets.

00:39:52.920 --> 00:39:56.170
And it should hopefully give you
enough information to get this

00:39:56.170 --> 00:39:58.417
up and running yourself.

00:39:58.417 --> 00:39:59.750
I'll let you all take a picture.

